## Observe grounding and search without changing answers

### What you will learn

Use real callbacks, distinguish snapshots from views, and recognize unfinished extensions.

### Observe a grounded choice rule

The observer sees ground rules. Its callback sends copied integers and a Boolean to the notebook process; those values remain valid after callback return. This observer records information and does not replace the solver. Compare these events with the [native ASPIF export](../elixir/04-inspection-and-export.livemd).

```elixir
parent = self()
tag = make_ref()

result =
  Clingo.with_control([models: :all], fn control ->
    Clingo.Control.register_observer!(control, %{
      rule: fn choice, head, body ->
        send(parent, {tag, choice, head, body})
        :ok
      end
    })

    Clingo.Control.add!(control, "{notice}.")
    Clingo.Control.ground!(control)
    Clingo.Control.solve!(control)
  end)

2 = length(result.models)

receive do
  {^tag, true, [_], []} -> :ok
after
  5_000 -> raise "Expected the observed choice rule"
end

Course.display(result)
```

### Inspect complete assignments without claiming propagation

The check callback runs on total assignments. It reads a decision level through a scoped view, checks the copied integer, and returns. This deliberately passive propagator leaves both choices available; a real constraint propagator must also detect conflicts or propagate consequences and handle undo. The control closes on exceptions too. Native views expire when the hook returns; retain only copied values, and never wait synchronously for an ordinary call on the same control from a hook.

```elixir
result =
  Clingo.with_control([models: :all], fn control ->
    Clingo.Control.register_propagator!(control, %{
      init: fn init -> Clingo.View.check_mode!(init, :clingo_propagator_check_mode_total) end,
      check: fn view ->
        level = Clingo.View.decision_level(Clingo.View.assignment(view))
        true = is_integer(level)
        :ok
      end
    })

    Clingo.Control.add!(control, "{notice}.")
    Clingo.Control.ground!(control)
    Clingo.Control.solve!(control)
  end)

2 = length(result.models)
Course.display(result)
```

### Try it yourself

Rename notice to event in both passive callback examples. Should observing or checking change the number of answers?

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

Both still enumerate two models. These callbacks inspect and report; they do not add clauses or propagate consequences. Scope views must not escape the callback.

</details>

## Observe search and add constraints on demand

Exercise files: [example.lp](../assets/propagators/example.lp) · [latin.lp](../assets/propagators/latin.lp) · [one.lp](../assets/propagators/one.lp) · [two.lp](../assets/propagators/two.lp).

```elixir
workdir =
  Course.workspace(Path.expand("../assets/propagators", __DIR__))
```

Start with symbolic-atom inspection, then follow assignment changes through a propagator. A symbolic atom has a program literal; initialization can map that literal to a solver literal. Several program literals may share a solver literal, so preserve that distinction when displaying assignments.

Extend the skeleton to trace propagation, undo and consistency checks, then experiment with a decision policy. The reference interactive tool is reference-only; the native wrapper supplies the corresponding extension points.

```elixir
Course.read(workdir, "example.lp") |> IO.puts()
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

### Map the initial assignment

Display each symbolic atom’s fact/external flags, its program literal and its mapped solver literal. Do not assume a one-to-one mapping at every boundary. Group atoms that share a solver literal when presenting the initial trail.

### Trace search changes

Report the decision level and newly assigned literals during propagation, record undo events when backtracking, and inspect candidates during checks. A conflict can return search to an earlier level immediately after a decision.

### Experiment with decisions

Choose an unassigned solver literal in the decision callback or return the supplied fallback. Implement any Livebook interaction without blocking a callback on another ordinary call in the same session.

```elixir
Course.read(workdir, "one.lp") |> IO.puts()
```

```elixir
Course.read(workdir, "two.lp") |> IO.puts()
```

### Specialize the trace to Latin squares

Render true `latin/3` atoms as a grid at each callback. A size-3 Latin square has 12 completions; fixing the top-left value to 1 leaves four. The supplied `latin.lp` includes that restriction. Build a trace from the live callbacks.

```elixir
Course.read(workdir, "latin.lp") |> IO.puts()
```

```elixir
Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "latin.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
items = ["latin(1,1,1)", "latin(2,2,2)", "latin(2,3,1)", "latin(3,2,1)", "latin(3,3,3)"]

grid =
  Map.new(items, fn item ->
    [x, y, n] = Regex.run(~r/latin\((\d+),(\d+),(\d+)\)/, item, capture: :all_but_first)
    {{String.to_integer(x), String.to_integer(y)}, n}
  end)

for x <- 1..3 do
  Enum.map_join(1..3, " ", &Map.get(grid, {x, &1}, ".")) |> IO.puts()
end
```

### Generate nogoods when needed

Instead of grounding every column restriction, detect duplicate values in a column and add the nogood excluding that combination. Compare checking only complete candidates, detecting conflicts during propagation, and propagating a forced exclusion before a conflict occurs. Always handle undo correctly.

### Compare three checking policies

First add a column-duplicate nogood only when checking a candidate. Then detect the same conflict during propagation. Finally, when a value is already present in a column, add the nogood that can exclude an unassigned duplicate immediately. The policies should agree on complete solution sets.

