## Start a solver-side agent

Exercise files: [example.lp](../assets/search-agent/example.lp).

```elixir
workdir = Course.workspace(Path.expand("../assets/search-agent", __DIR__))
```

This exercise starts a custom search agent through propagator callbacks. The native Elixir skeleton registers initialization, decision and consistency hooks, then loads and solves the example program. It currently defers decisions to the solver and only reports a decision level.

Add the required watches, deductions and decisions to implement an actual agent. Keep callback work bounded, respect callback-scoped views and use the wrapper’s propagator API. A completed solve of the skeleton is not evidence that the missing agent behavior exists.

```elixir
Course.read(workdir, "example.lp") |> IO.puts()
```

```elixir
Clingo.with_control(fn control ->
  # Original agent skeleton: fill in watches/decisions as the exercise asks.
  propagator = %{
    init: fn _init -> :ok end,
    decide: fn _thread, _assignment, fallback -> fallback end,
    check: fn propagation ->
      assignment = Clingo.View.assignment(propagation)
      IO.inspect(Clingo.View.decision_level(assignment), label: "decision level")
      :ok
    end
  }

  Control.register_propagator!(control, propagator)
  Control.load!(control, Path.join(workdir, "example.lp"))
  Control.ground!(control)
  Control.solve!(control)
end)
```

